"""Original ecosocial evaluation teaching and invented numerical examples."""
def course(c,m):
    return c("SWP-048","Evaluating Ecosocial Interventions","evaluating-ecosocial-interventions","Evaluating Ecosocial\nInterventions",
        "Prepare a proportionate ecosocial evaluation brief with relevant questions, transparent indicators, equity-sensitive evidence, bounded conclusions and accountable follow-through.",[
m("Evaluation questions and evaluability","Select a useful evaluation question and identify the evidence and authority needed to answer it.",[
"Evaluation makes an explicit judgement about an intervention for a stated purpose, using relevant evidence and criteria. Routine monitoring can show whether activities are delivered and alert a team to a barrier; an evaluation asks a defined question about value, performance or consequences and explains how a judgement will be reached. Begin by identifying who needs the answer, what decision it will inform and whose experience matters. The social work education benchmark connects research-informed practice and evaluation, but does not accredit this course or authorise a local finding simply because the report uses an evaluation label. [S3, S1]",
"OECD guidance discusses relevance, coherence, effectiveness, efficiency, impact and sustainability as lenses to apply thoughtfully. Select and interpret criteria for the purpose and context instead of treating all six as a compulsory identical scoring checklist. Relevance might examine whether an arrangement addresses a priority; coherence might examine its fit with other services. These are questions, not automatic scores. Explain whose priorities and values inform the judgement and how disagreement will be recorded. Choosing an impact criterion does not provide the design or evidence needed to establish that the intervention caused a change. [S9, S1]",
"Map the working theory of change before choosing measures. Distinguish resources and activities from outputs and intended outcomes, and state assumptions that connect them. For an access arrangement, a published contact process is an output; people reaching suitable support is a different claim requiring evidence. Ask whether the required records, starting point, perspectives, skills, time and permissions are available. This evaluability check may show that the question should be narrowed or deferred. The participation review supports scrutiny of influence and missing perspectives; it does not guarantee that a convenient survey will answer every intended question. [R2, S9]",
"Use an evaluation matrix with purpose, criterion, question, intended claim, indicator or qualitative evidence, source, starting point, limitation and responsible role. Check that collection is necessary and proportionate, and identify the required ethical, organisational and community permissions before proceeding. A small review can produce useful bounded learning without claiming programme effectiveness for every group. State what the evaluation cannot answer and who may authorise changes in response. Where a question would need specialist causal analysis, technical measurement or legal judgement, identify that dependency rather than substitute a confident narrative for missing capability. [S9, S3, S1]"],
"Fictional case: a team wants to know whether a contact arrangement suits people facing access barriers. It selects a relevance question and a bounded effectiveness question, checks available starting-point records and requests accessible feedback. It does not call an attendance sheet an impact evaluation.",
"Draft an evaluation matrix for one access question. Include its intended decision, selected criterion, evidence source, assumption, missing perspective, permission and a claim that the proposed design cannot support.",
"A useful matrix connects a decision to an answerable question and identifies the evidence gap. The choice of a criterion or indicator cannot itself establish attribution, accreditation or an intervention's success.",["S3","S1","S9","R2"],[
("Evaluation needs a stated purpose and judgement question.","A team collects activity counts without stating what decision they inform. What should be clarified first?","The evaluation purpose, intended decision and question.",["The report's cover colour.","A conclusion of effectiveness before analysis.","A rule that more collected data always improves judgement."],"Useful evidence collection follows a defined purpose."),
("Evaluation criteria are contextual lenses.","A reviewer applies all six OECD criteria as an identical scoring checklist without context. What should change?","Select and interpret criteria according to purpose and context, explaining the choice.",["Assume each criterion provides a causal method.","Remove all explanation of whose priorities matter.","Treat a total score as proof of universal effectiveness."],"The guidance calls for thoughtful application rather than mechanical scoring."),
("Outputs and intended outcomes require distinct evidence.","An evaluation asks whether users reached suitable support. Which is only an output measure?","The number of contact guides distributed.",["Users' accounts of reaching appropriate support.","Verified access to the intended service.","Evidence about barriers at the next step."],"Distribution records show production or reach, not necessarily successful access."),
("Evaluability checks whether a question can be answered responsibly.","A proposed evaluation lacks baseline records, permissions and required analytical skills. What is appropriate?","Identify those gaps and narrow or defer unsupported claims while resolving them.",["Invent a starting point.","Proceed as if resources were confirmed.","Declare that a report template supplies the missing evidence."],"An explicit feasibility check prevents conclusions beyond available evidence."),
("Choosing an impact criterion does not establish causality.","A report is titled impact evaluation but uses only an uncontrolled post-activity survey. What must its claims reflect?","The design's limited ability to attribute observed outcomes to the activity.",["Causality guaranteed by the title.","Proof of effects for all non-respondents.","Automatic equivalence to a controlled study."],"The method and evidence, rather than the label, bound attribution."),
]),
m("Design evidence and equity","Interpret indicators and diverse accounts with transparent denominators and causal limits.",[
"Match the evidence approach to the evaluation question. Counts can describe recorded activity; interviews or accessible feedback can explore how an arrangement is experienced. Neither method is automatically more valid for every purpose, and combining methods does not remove their individual limitations. Specify who is included, how information will be obtained, the relevant period and what a missing response means. Review whether the approach excludes people most affected by the barrier. The participation review highlights power and whose voice shapes findings, while a local evaluation still needs transparent recruitment and an honest account of absent perspectives. [R2, S1, S9]",
"Define every rate with a numerator, denominator, time period and population. In an invented exercise, twelve of twenty respondents report access at the starting point and eighteen of thirty report access at review. Both recorded proportions are sixty percent, although the second count is larger. These are fictional teaching figures, not SWEEP learner or community data. The groups may differ and respondents may not represent everyone. Report the counts and proportions with those limitations; do not claim a higher access rate from the larger numerator or infer that the intervention caused a change. [S9, S1]",
"Causal questions require a design able to examine what would likely have happened otherwise, with assumptions and limitations made explicit by appropriate expertise. A simple before-and-after comparison can be influenced by changing weather, service capacity, demand or other interventions. A comparison group can also differ in important ways and is not automatically a valid counterfactual. This introductory course asks learners to identify those concerns, not to implement an unreviewed experimental study. Where only descriptive evidence is feasible, state that scope clearly and use it for the question it can actually answer. [S9, S3]",
"Include social effects and trade-offs when selecting ecosocial indicators. Horneman and colleagues review indicators in coastal and transitional nature-based adaptation cases and identify uneven coverage, including limited socio-economic attention. Their literature search ends in June 2023 despite the paper's 2026 publication. It offers reasons to examine indicator balance, not a universal metric set or proof of a local ecological effect. An environmental output can coexist with reduced access or increased unpaid work for some people. Seek relevant perspectives and qualified technical interpretation rather than using one favourable indicator to represent every consequence. [R10, R2, S1]"],
"Fictional case: access is reported by twelve of twenty respondents before an activity and eighteen of thirty afterwards. Both proportions are sixty percent. The evaluator also hears from people excluded by the contact format and notes that the respondent groups differ, so neither a rate improvement nor causation is established.",
"Calculate both proportions and write a four-sentence findings note. Include numerator and denominator, the unchanged rate, one missing-perspective concern and one reason the design cannot attribute an effect. Add a social trade-off question for an environmental activity.",
"Both proportions equal sixty percent. A larger count is not a higher rate; different respondents, missing perspectives and other influences limit comparison. A balanced ecosocial assessment examines social consequences as well as environmental outputs.",["R2","S1","S9","S3","R10"],[
("Mixed methods retain their individual limitations.","An evaluation combines a convenience survey and a few interviews. What should it still report?","Selection, missing perspectives and other limits of each evidence source.",["That combining methods guarantees representation.","That every causal question is now resolved.","That interview findings apply numerically to the entire population."],"Combining sources can add insight without eliminating design limitations."),
("Rates need explicit denominators.","In the exercise, access is reported by 12 of 20 people and later 18 of 30. Which interpretation is correct?","Both recorded proportions are 60 percent; the larger count does not show a higher rate.",["The rate increased because eighteen is greater than twelve.","The later rate is 90 percent because the earlier denominator should be reused.","The figures prove the intervention caused improved access."],"Twelve divided by twenty and eighteen divided by thirty both equal 0.6."),
("Missing responses can affect interpretation.","People unable to use the contact format are absent from feedback. What should the evaluator do?","Report the exclusion and consider an appropriate accessible way to hear their experience.",["Assume their experience matches respondents exactly.","Delete the limitation from the findings.","Treat silence as satisfaction."],"An excluded perspective may matter directly to the access question."),
("A comparison group is not automatically a valid counterfactual.","Two communities differ in service capacity before a programme. What concern affects a simple outcome comparison?","Baseline differences may explain part of the observed difference.",["Any second community guarantees causal attribution.","Service capacity is irrelevant by definition.","A comparison cancels every measurement problem."],"A credible causal interpretation needs design assumptions and appropriate expertise."),
("Environmental indicators can omit social trade-offs.","An adaptation report measures vegetation but omits users' access and unpaid workload. What should be considered?","Relevant social outcomes and burdens alongside the environmental measures.",["A rule that vegetation represents every consequence.","A universal indicator set inferred from one review.","A guarantee that environmental outputs benefit everyone equally."],"An ecosocial judgement needs a relevant balance of consequences."),
]),
m("Judgement use and follow-through","Write a bounded judgement and an accountable response without overstating efficiency or effect.",[
"An evaluation judgement should show the question, relevant criterion, evidence, limitations and reasoning that connect them. Separate an observation from an interpretation and distinguish an unmeasured outcome from a negative result. Findings may be mixed across groups and time periods, and disagreement may identify a real difference in priorities rather than an error to average away. State the strength of the supporting evidence in ordinary language. The OECD guidance helps organise evaluative questions, while professional responsibility requires that the wording of the conclusion remains proportionate to what the actual design and sources can demonstrate. [S9, S1, S3]",
"Efficiency questions need transparent information about resources and the relevant result. In an invented exercise, forty planning units support ten recorded participants, giving four units per participant. This is not a real Nigerian price, a health cost-effectiveness ratio or evidence that the arrangement is equitable. Unpaid time, accessibility costs, quality and benefits may be absent from the simple calculation. A comparison also needs sufficiently comparable costs, periods, participants and outcomes. Explain what the arithmetic includes and omits before using it to inform a decision; a lower ratio alone cannot establish a better intervention. [S9, S1]",
"Connect findings to a response that a responsible role can authorise. A recommendation should name the concern, proposed action, owner, dependency and review point, with an alternative if resources or authority remain unresolved. Contributors should receive an accessible account of the findings and the response, within the agreed knowledge and privacy arrangements. The participation review supports examining whether feedback has influence, not simply whether it was collected. Keep identifying details out of public summaries where people could be recognised or harmed, and record a route to question an interpretation or raise a correction. [R2, S1, S9]",
"Preserve an audit trail of the evidence basis, report version, decision and later amendment. If a technical indicator is uncertain, seek qualified interpretation and state that uncertainty rather than substitute a success label. The nature-based-solutions review supports careful indicator selection but does not certify this intervention's effectiveness. A proportionate evaluation may recommend continuing, revising or gathering better evidence, depending on the question and authority. Review whether the agreed response actually occurred and what remains unanswered. An evaluation report can support learning without acting as a professional accreditation, a school examination pass or a certificate authorisation. [R10, S3, S1, S9]"],
"Fictional case: forty exercise units support ten participants. A report notes four units per participant but missing unpaid time and outcome evidence. Users report a useful contact process alongside an access barrier. The authorised owner agrees a revision and review date, and the report retains both findings and its limitations.",
"Write a short judgement and response note. Show the exercise ratio and its omissions, one supported finding, one unmeasured outcome, an action owner, a dependency, a feedback route and a review date.",
"Four units per participant is an arithmetic description. A sound judgement explains missing costs and outcomes, preserves mixed findings and gives an authorised response route without claiming proven effectiveness or certification.",["S9","S1","S3","R2","R10"],[
("An unmeasured outcome is not a demonstrated negative outcome.","A review has no wellbeing data. Which wording is defensible?","Wellbeing effects were not established by this evidence.",["The intervention definitely worsened wellbeing.","The intervention definitely improved wellbeing.","No measurement is needed because a report exists."],"Absence of measurement cannot establish an effect's direction."),
("Simple resource ratios have a bounded meaning.","Forty fictional units support ten recorded participants. What does four units per participant establish?","A descriptive ratio with costs, quality, equity and outcomes still to assess.",["A real Nigerian market price.","A proven health cost-effectiveness result.","The best possible intervention regardless of outcomes."],"The calculation does not contain the information needed for broader value judgements."),
("Recommendations require responsibility and authority.","An evaluation recommends an access revision with no owner or confirmed resources. What should be added?","A responsible role, authority or resource dependency and review point.",["A claim that publication makes implementation automatic.","An assumption that another service has accepted the task.","An instruction to hide the unresolved dependency."],"A usable recommendation makes its route to action explicit."),
("Mixed findings should remain visible.","Feedback identifies a useful process and a persistent access barrier. How should the conclusion be framed?","Describe both findings with their evidence and limitations.",["Remove the barrier to keep a positive narrative.","Ignore the useful process to make every finding negative.","Average the statements into an unsupported universal claim."],"Balanced reporting retains relevant differences and unresolved concerns."),
("Evaluation supports learning without authorising credentials.","A team completes an ecosocial evaluation report. What does this alone authorise?","No course pass or school certificate; those require their separate verified processes.",["Automatic certification for every participant.","Replacement of the final ParaLearn school examination.","Professional accreditation of the intervention."],"An evaluation finding is separate from assessment and credential authorisation."),
]),
])
